ion  um and your limited quantification your  precision accuracy  how how robust is your method what is  your  your range um how how low you go to how  high you can see as far as the amounts  within your samples  so there's a lot of different components  within method validation that you'll  have to  really kind of keep in mind as you're  setting up a validation plan  and there's a lot of different resources  out there to be able to  really kind of get all the different  components together  it's really important to take time and  really kind of walk through this  validation  um plan slowly  and um keep notes  and document everything that you are  doing and all of the  uh the data um so you have it in one  spot to be able to  put together a package to really show  your method is  is up and running at a high quality  so once you kind of have your um  methods and your instrumentation and  everything up and running and everything  is  is validated you're ready to actually  start  the uh client-facing portion  of the analytical testing this is really  where it comes down to your sample  management  how do you get samples what is the  workflow within your lab  the all-important traceability and then  your reagents and your consumables and  how  those kind of interplay within all of  the levels  of your sample management so to do this  a lot of times you need technologies or  softwares to kind of help integrate  within your laboratory  a process to to make this smooth  and pretty seamless a lot of times you  use what's called  a limb system or a laboratory  information management program  or system and  so this is really kind of the the  heartbeat  of an analytical lab and this is where  a lot of times it is required by your  government bodies  if you are a regulatory lab  and limbs can be used for a lot of  different things within a lab and  different lens programs have a lot of  different sections or different  components that that can be used  and some of them are real